he START date/time is the first dose. When I changed start time to 2:00 AM with night owl ON, the START DATE shifted — this is the comparison logic: picking a time relative to stored date. The potential bug: picking 2:00 AM with night owl ON changed the stored start date from Nov 14 to Nov 15 (displayed Nov 14). Then toggling night owl off revealed Nov 15. A user picking 2:00 AM as their first dose on Nov 14 would find their group starting Nov 15 (or 13?). That's a data-changing side effect from the picker. This might be the intended bug.\n\nActually let me test precisely: fresh group, night owl ON, set start time 2:00 AM, start date Nov 14. Save. Then check Plan summary \"Since Nov 14\" or \"Since Nov 15\"? That determines whether the start date is wrongly stored. Let's test after reverting current group to daily etc. Actually easier: create a second group to avoid disturbing Group 1? Hmm. But data consistency in Plan would then have 2 groups. That's fine for testing, and I can delete later if the app allows.\n\nActually, we can just edit Group 1: turn night owl ON, set start time 2:00 AM, save, check \"Since\" date. Then revert.\n\nHmm, but \"first-dose picker\" comparisons: let me test setting the START DATE with night owl ON after picking 2:00 AM. I'll do a clean sequence:\n1. Edit Group 1 (weekly currently, Mon, 8:00 AM).\n2. Turn night owl ON.\n3. Pick START TIME 2:00 AM. Observe START DATE.\n4. Pick START DATE Nov 14 (tap, maybe date picker, select 14, OK). Observe if start time/date stay consistent.\n5. Save; check Plan \"Since\".\n\nLet me do it."}
