{"type": "thinking", "text": "Preview shows only 10:00 AM (the new time). So the \"8:00 AM → 10:00 AM\" row is display-only showing an old value plus the new one; the actual schedule uses 10:00. This is a display bug: the TIME row shows a stale extra time. It says \"TIME\" singular but shows a range, and this mismatches the preview. That's reportable: wrong value / stale display after changing the weekly dose time.\n\nHmm, but hold on: maybe the row \"8:00 AM → 10:00 AM\" is intentionally showing the previous value → new value as a \"change preview\"? No, that's absurd for a saved form.\n\nNow: is this specific to Weekly mode or does it also happen in Daily? In Daily we saw correct replacement. So it's Weekly-only. Hmm, that's odd for the changed bullet \"first-dose picker\". Maybe the \"first dose\" is the weekly TIME (the single time for the selected days)? Eh.\n\nBefore reporting, let me consider another possible explanation: The weekly TIME row might represent the RANGE of times-of-day when there are multiple times in the DAILY list. Wait, daily list currently has [6:00 AM, 8:00 PM]? No — after discard, the saved group is weekly [8:00]; switching to daily earlier showed 9:00 (then I added 8 PM, then edited 6 AM, all discarded). After discard and reopen, daily list might be [9:00]? We didn't check. But the weekly row showed 8:00 before edit, then 8:00→10:00. So no.\n\nAlright, let me also test whether this bug affects the \"first-dose\" START TIME picker (i.e., the date/time shifting) — maybe the real injected bug is there and the weekly range is \"normal\" (unlikely).\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe I should think about the weekly TIME row differently: In weekly mode, the app might offer multiple times per day too (though no Add). But it shows a range because it's a LIST of 2 after edit. The fact that preview uses only 10:00 suggests the extra 8:00 is an artifact. The user sees \"8:00 AM → 10:00 AM\" but the schedule is 10:00 — misleading, a bug.\n\nHold on, maybe the range display is actually \"first dose time → recurring time\" where first dose is stored separately from the weekly time — and the first dose is 8:00 because the group was created at 7:00... no, it shows 8:00 which was the previous weekly time. So it's the previous value.\n\nOK. Let me also test the START TIME picker for a similar artifact: changing START TIME from 5:00 to another time didn't create a range (START TIME showed a single value). So the artifact is specific to the TIME (first-dose?) picker — matching the changed bullet \"Group editor first-dose picker: a picked value is now compared with the stored one before it is applied.\" Perhaps the bug: the picker compares picked with stored and, if different, appends a new time instead of replacing. That matches: TIME row = list of times; the picker for the \"first dose\" (first time in TIMES OF DAY) appends. And in Daily mode, the picker replaced correctly... Hmm, but the Daily list row edit was the same code? Maybe not.\n\nWait, in Weekly mode the TIME row is a single \"first dose\" (the dose time for the selected weekdays). Editing it in weekly mode appends. In Daily mode, the row is each time-of-day; editing replaces. So different code paths. The changed bullet specifically says \"Group editor first-dose picker\" — the weekly TIME row could be exactly the \"first-dose picker\"! Hmm, not sure.\n\nActually, \"first-dose picker\" makes more sense for the START TIME (the first dose). But the START TIME picker behaved fine.\n\nHmm, let me test another scenario with the START TIME picker: picking a time when night owl is ON with a start time before 04:00 and stored time also before 04:00. E.g., group start 2:00 AM, night owl ON. Now pick 3:00 AM. Does the start date shift again? Each edit may bump the date. Let's test: enable night owl, set start time 2:00 AM (start date becomes...), then pick 3:00 AM, watch date.\n\nActually earlier sequence: night owl ON + 2:00 AM → date Nov 14 (displayed). Then toggle off → Nov 15. 