{"type": "thinking", "text": "Still broken. This may be an intentional environment hiccup or the app crashed. Hmm.\n\nLet me consider: the time picker dialog is open, and my tap on element 11 may have triggered a crash? The BrokenPipe suggests the device automation server died, not necessarily the app.\n\nLet me retry after some seconds. Meanwhile, think about the START TIME reset to 12:00 AM after changing the date. This is a strong candidate bug: changing the START DATE resets START TIME to 12:00 AM. But wait — could this be intended? If the picked date+time (Nov 20 11:00) is in the future, no reason to reset. The app reset the time to 12:00 AM when the date changed. Actually, hmm: maybe the reset happened because the previous time was 11:00 AM and after picking the date, the time shown \"12:00 AM\" comes from a date-only picker (time not preserved). That's a genuine bug: picking a start date wipes the start time.\n\nWait, but earlier when I picked the SAME date (Nov 14) from the picker, the time remained 5:00 AM. So picking the same date doesn't reset; picking a DIFFERENT date resets to 12:00 AM. That matches \"picked value compared with the stored one before it is applied\": if the date equals stored, keep; else apply the new date but reset the time. Hmm, maybe the injected bug is the time reset.\n\nHold on, actually maybe the reset is because the app sets the first-dose datetime to the start of the picked day (midnight) when the date changes, and then the user is expected to pick the time. But the UI doesn't communicate that; it's still wrong for preservation. However, this may be intentional: choosing a new start date resets the time to the beginning of that day because the time part is a separate picker. Hmm.\n\nActually, if I pick Nov 20 with time 11:00 and then the time resets to 12:00 AM, that's bad UX but might be \"expected\" if the date picker only picks a date and the time defaults to 00:00. But the previous time was preserved when the date was unchanged — suggests the compare logic.\n\nI need more tests. Let me wait for the device to come back.\n\nLet me retry."}
